--- !u!114 &697368396
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 697368394}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b90801c2cf1d0fb4bab16cab9945589f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nvRamInfo: '<b>Function:</b>

    NvRAM is a type of memory that retains data even
    after the power is turned off. It is often used in mission-critical applications
    where data integrity is essential, such as caching and logging.



    <b>Key
    Features:</b>


    Persistence: Retains stored data even during power loss.


    Speed:
    Faster than traditional SSDs but slower than volatile RAM (DRAM).


    Usage:
    Common in database servers, high-speed caching, and write-back buffers.


    <b>Types:</b>


    Battery-backed
    RAM (BBRAM): Uses a battery to preserve data.


    Flash-based NvRAM: Stores
    data in non-volatile flash memory without requiring a battery.'
  dfmInfo: "<b>Function:</b>\r\nA Direct Flash Module (DFM) is a high-performance,
    NVMe-based flash storage solution designed for enterprise servers. Unlike traditional
    SSDs, DFMs connect directly to the motherboard or storage backplane, reducing
    latency and improving data throughput.\r\n\r\n<b>Key Features:</b>\r\n\r\nLow
    Latency: Eliminates bottlenecks associated with SATA/SAS interfaces, providing
    faster read/write speeds.\r\n\r\nHigh Reliability: Designed for enterprise workloads,
    ensuring data integrity and endurance.\r\n\r\nCompact Form Factor: Often comes
    in EDSFF (Enterprise & Data Center SSD Form Factor) for efficient server integration.\r\n\r\nPower
    Efficiency: Optimized for sustained performance with lower power consumption
    compared to traditional SSDs.\r\n\r\nHot-Swappable: Some DFMs support hot-swapping,
    allowing replacements without shutting down the system.\r\n\r\n<b>Use Cases:</b>\r\n\r\nHigh-speed
    database applications (e.g., SQL, NoSQL).\r\n\r\nVirtualized environments requiring
    fast storage access.\r\n\r\nAI/ML workloads that demand rapid data processing.\r\n\r\nCloud
    and hyperscale data centers for efficient storage scaling.\r"
  psuInfo: "<b>Function:</b>\r\nThe Power Supply Unit (PSU) converts electrical power
    from an external source into the required voltage and current to operate server
    components. It ensures stable and uninterrupted power delivery.\r\n\r\n<b>Types:</b>\r\n\r\nSingle
    PSU: One power supply unit, vulnerable to failures.\r\n\r\nRedundant PSU: Two
    or more power supplies working together for reliability; if one fails, the other
    takes over.\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>Key Considerations:</b>\r\n\r\nWattage: Higher wattage
    supports more powerful components.\r\n\r\nEfficiency Rating: Certifications like
    80 PLUS Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum, Titanium indicate energy efficiency.\r\n\r\nHot-Swap
    Capability: Some PSUs can be replaced while the server is running.\r"
  chassisInfo: '<b>Function:</b>

    The chassis houses all server components, providing
    structural support, cooling, and accessibility for maintenance.


    <b>Types:</b>


    Tower
    Server: Resembles a desktop PC, used for small businesses or remote offices.


    Rack
    Server: Designed for mounting in a data center rack (1U, 2U, 4U sizes).


    Blade
    Server: Compact, modular design that shares power and cooling with other blades
    in a chassis.


    <b>Key Considerations:</b>


    Size & Form Factor: Determines
    how many servers fit in a rack.


    Cooling & Airflow: Servers generate heat,
    requiring efficient cooling solutions.


    Expandability: Space for additional
    drives, GPUs, and network cards.'
  flashArray: 
